'' AN ENUM AND ITS UNDERLYING INTEGER COST THE SAME, so any overload set holding both
'' TIED -- the call bound to neither, came out UNTYPED, and whatever consumed it
'' declined. Never at the call, which is what made it hard to see: adding an ENUM
'' overload broke resolution for the INTEGER one beside it. overload/simple.bas is
'' seven of those.
''
'' THE COST IS NOT SYMMETRIC, and every tier here was measured by RUNNING fbc:
''
''   int argument  vs { integer, E }    takes integer
''   E   argument  vs { integer, E }    takes E
''   int argument  vs { longint, E }    takes longint
''   int argument  vs { byte,    E }    takes byte
''   int argument  vs { single,  E }    takes single
''   int argument  vs { boolean, E }    takes boolean
''   int argument  vs { E }             ACCEPTED -- so it is a cost, not a refusal
''   E   argument  vs { integer, byte } takes integer
''   E   argument  vs { single, integer } takes integer
''
'' So a NUMERIC argument reaching an ENUM parameter is the dearest conversion short of
'' a user-defined one, and an ENUM argument reaching a numeric parameter behaves as an
'' INTEGER does -- but neither is EXACT, because an exact match on the enum itself has
'' to beat both.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/enum-overload-cost.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: _Z2P21E
'' GOLDEN: expected/enum-overload-cost.out
